export const articles=[
 {slug:'plastering-cost-leeds',category:'Costs & Pricing',title:'How much does plastering cost in Leeds?',excerpt:'What changes a quote, what to compare and why a room price on its own rarely tells the whole story.',image:'kc01',answer:'The useful number is a quote for your actual surfaces, preparation and access—not an unsupported price per room. Lyons Interiors offers free consultations and project quotes. No fixed price list has been published here.',sections:[
 ['What are you actually paying for?','Start with the scope. A sound wall needing a finishing skim is a different job from a wall with damaged backing, a ceiling requiring investigation or a room with several awkward returns. Two rooms with the same floor area can have very different amounts of wall and ceiling to finish.','Ask your plasterer to separate the surfaces being worked on from the preparation those surfaces need. That distinction makes a quote easier to understand and much easier to compare.'],
 ['The details that change the price','The area to be finished is only the starting point. Existing wallpaper, loose coatings, damaged plaster, ceiling height, access and the amount of protection or moving required can change the work involved. Whether you want one wall, all walls or walls and ceiling should be explicit.','Scheduling can also affect what is practical. A vacant room with clear access is a different working arrangement from a busy space shared with other trades. Explain the actual situation rather than trying to simplify it for a cheaper first estimate.'],
 ['Compare the same job, not just the bottom line','Ask whether preparation, materials, protection and waste arrangements are included. Check whether VAT applies and whether the stated total includes it. Painting, electrical alterations and moving fitted items should not be assumed to be part of plastering.','If one quote is much lower, ask what approach or scope explains the difference. It may be a perfectly suitable smaller job—or an estimate that leaves important work out. Price alone cannot tell you which.'],
 ['How to get a useful quote','Send a wide photograph of each surface, close-ups of visible damage, rough measurements, your postcode and the stage of your project. Say what you want finished and what other work is planned. A site visit may still be needed to assess the background.','Before accepting, get the agreed scope, total, any payment stages and the process for additional work recorded. Do not rely on a headline figure whose assumptions have never been discussed.']
 ],takeaway:'Ask for a clear scope before choosing a price. A free consultation is the next step towards a project-specific figure.',related:['skimming-vs-replastering','choosing-a-plasterer','what-to-send-for-a-quote']},
 {slug:'skimming-vs-replastering',category:'Comparisons & Options',title:'Skimming or replastering: what do your walls actually need?',excerpt:'A fresh surface and a rebuilt background are not the same job. Here is how to have the right conversation.',image:'kc02',answer:'Skimming is a finishing layer over a suitable background. More extensive replastering may be needed when the existing background is not sound. The right choice starts with the condition of the wall.',sections:[
 ['Why the terminology gets confusing','People often use “plastering” to mean any work involving plaster. One quote may use it to describe a skim; another may include removing failed material and preparing a new background. Instead of comparing labels, ask what happens to the existing surface and what layers the proposed work includes.'],
 ['When a fresh skim may be the conversation','If you dislike the appearance of otherwise suitable walls, a finishing skim may be worth discussing. Examples include extensive cosmetic patchwork or a surface that does not suit the finish you want. Tell the plasterer what is currently on the wall and whether you have noticed looseness, damp patches or changing cracks.','A photograph helps explain the appearance, but it cannot reliably establish adhesion or the condition behind every coating. Allow for an inspection before treating an initial conversation as a final specification.'],
 ['When the background needs attention first','Loose or failing material, persistent moisture and continuing movement should not simply be covered to improve appearance. The cause needs understanding and an appropriate plan. British Gypsum’s technical guidance highlights the importance of adhesion, compatible preparation and moisture when diagnosing finish failures.'],
 ['Questions to ask before saying yes','Ask: Is the existing background suitable? What preparation is included? Are any damaged areas being removed or rebuilt? What is excluded? What would change the scope once work starts?','A smaller repair can be sensible where damage is genuinely limited. A wider finish can make more sense when many patches would interrupt the same wall. Ask for the reasoning behind the recommendation, not just the name of the service.']
 ],takeaway:'The right solution is the smallest appropriate scope that gives you the finish you need—not automatically a skim or a complete strip-out.',refs:['gypsum'],related:['patch-repair-or-reskim','plastering-cost-leeds']},
 {slug:'how-messy-is-plastering',category:'Problems & Risks',title:'How messy is plastering—and how should you prepare?',excerpt:'An honest look at disruption, protection and the conversations worth having before work starts.',image:'kc03',answer:'Plastering is building work, so some disruption is part of the job. The useful question is how the work area, access route, belongings and clean-up will be managed—not whether somebody promises zero mess.',sections:[
 ['Get specific about the work area','Tell your plasterer whether the room is empty, partly furnished or in everyday use. Discuss large furniture, fitted items and flooring before the booking. Avoid assuming everything can be moved into the centre: access to walls and ceilings may require a different arrangement.','Also consider the route from the entrance to the room. Hallways, stairs and doorways form part of the working area, even when they are not being plastered.'],
 ['Agree what “tidy” means','Ask what protection is included, who moves belongings, where materials can be kept and how waste will be handled. Confirm access to water and where tools can be cleaned. A practical conversation about these details is more valuable than an undefined promise of a spotless job.','A tradesperson’s end-of-job tidy and a deep domestic clean are not necessarily the same thing. Make your expectations clear so neither side is guessing.'],
 ['Living at home while the work happens','Explain whether you will be working from home, using adjoining rooms or relying on one main route through the property. Plan to keep children and pets out of the work area. For a larger refurbishment, ask whether separate stages are practical, understanding that staging can affect the programme and price.'],
 ['Make the handover part of the plan','Before work starts, discuss when the room can be used again, what still needs drying and what your decorator needs to know. Do not book decorating solely from the plasterer’s final working day. These are separate milestones.','Our room-preparation checklist gives you a simple starting point to discuss with whoever is doing your work.']
 ],takeaway:'A clear protection and access plan reduces surprises. Agree responsibilities before moving furniture or booking a start date.',related:['prepare-a-room-for-plastering','planning-your-plastering-project']},
 {slug:'when-can-you-paint-new-plaster',category:'Planning & Aftercare',title:'When can you paint new plaster?',excerpt:'Why “the plasterer has finished” and “ready to decorate” are two different moments.',image:'kc04',answer:'Wait until the plaster is suitably dry and follow the plaster and paint manufacturers’ instructions. A calendar estimate is not a substitute for checking the actual surface and conditions.',sections:[
 ['Do not confuse a finished job with a dry wall','Ask about drying and decorating before you book your painter. The plastering work can be complete while moisture remains in the surface or background. British Gypsum identifies residual moisture and premature painting among the possible contributors to delamination.','There is no fixed drying promise on this website: the product, depth, background and room conditions need to be considered for your project.'],
 ['Build some flexibility into your schedule','Let your decorator know what work has been done and which products were used. Arrange a check rather than assuming that a particular day after plastering will always be suitable. This matters particularly when a job contains both thin finishing work and deeper repairs.','If you are coordinating several trades, treat “plastering complete” and “decoration can begin” as separate steps in the programme.'],
 ['Ask for product-specific preparation','The correct first coat depends on the chosen paint system and surface. Follow the paint manufacturer’s instructions for new plaster, including any recommended primer or dilution. Do not use a universal water-to-paint ratio copied from an unrelated product.','Ask your decorator to explain the proposed first coat and why it is suitable. Keep the product information alongside the details of the plastering work.'],
 ['What if a patch still looks different?','Send your plasterer and decorator a photograph and describe the room conditions. A photograph alone cannot confirm the cause or dryness. Do not hide an unexplained damp patch behind paint simply to keep a deadline.','Where there is a known leak or another continuing moisture source, that needs resolving separately rather than being treated as ordinary drying.']
 ],takeaway:'Plan the decorating around the wall’s readiness and the product instructions, not a guaranteed number of days.',refs:['gypsum'],related:['planning-your-plastering-project','what-to-send-for-a-quote']},
 {slug:'prepare-a-room-for-plastering',category:'Planning & Aftercare',title:'Your room-preparation checklist for plastering',excerpt:'What to discuss, what to move and what not to disturb before your plasterer arrives.',image:'kc05',answer:'Agree the preparation responsibilities first. Clear belongings and access only as advised, and do not disturb an unknown ceiling coating or carry out electrical alterations yourself.',sections:[
 ['Before you start moving everything','Confirm which walls and ceilings are included. Ask whether the room must be empty and who is responsible for moving heavy items. Mention fitted wardrobes, large beds, delicate flooring and any items that cannot be moved. Photograph the room so the access arrangements can be discussed in advance.'],
 ['Make a simple room plan','Remove small, fragile and valuable belongings from the agreed work area. Discuss curtains, blinds, pictures and shelves rather than assuming everything can remain. Keep an accessible route through the property and agree where materials may be stored.','If radiators, sockets or other services need attention, agree who will carry out that work and when. Use an appropriately competent trade where necessary; do not improvise to save preparation time.'],
 ['Check the practical details','Confirm parking or unloading arrangements, access times, water availability and any building restrictions. Explain arrangements for keys, alarms, children and pets. For flats or shared buildings, mention communal areas and any restrictions imposed by the building manager.'],
 ['Do not turn preparation into an accidental bigger job','Do not scrape or sand a suspect textured coating. Do not begin stripping surfaces simply because you assume it will reduce the bill. The plasterer may need to see the existing condition first, and older finishes can require assessment.','Use the consultation to decide what preparation you can safely take responsibility for and what should remain within the agreed professional scope.'],
 ['A final check before the date','Confirm the scope and quote, the arrival arrangements, how changes will be agreed and the intended handover. Keep the contact number handy. A short written checklist helps both sides start with the same expectations.']
 ],takeaway:'Clear the room by agreement—not by guesswork. Good preparation starts with a conversation.',related:['how-messy-is-plastering','textured-ceilings-and-asbestos']},
 {slug:'textured-ceilings-and-asbestos',category:'Problems & Risks',title:'Can you plaster over a textured or Artex ceiling?',excerpt:'Before choosing a smooth finish, understand why an older ceiling may need specialist assessment.',image:'kc06',answer:'Sometimes a smooth finish may be possible, but the existing coating and background must be assessed first. Older textured coatings may contain asbestos. Do not scrape, sand, drill or otherwise disturb a suspect surface to investigate it yourself.',sections:[
 ['Start with what is on the ceiling','“Artex” is often used as shorthand for a textured ceiling. The appearance does not establish the full composition, age or condition of the material. HSE identifies textured decorative coatings among the locations where asbestos can be found.','Give your contractor any survey or test information already available. If the material is uncertain, explain that clearly rather than describing it as asbestos-free.'],
 ['Why a photograph is not a clearance','Photographs are useful for showing the room, pattern and visible condition. They are not a substitute for an appropriate assessment or analysis. Where asbestos is suspected, seek advice from a competent asbestos professional before planning work that could disturb the coating.','HSE says work on asbestos requires suitable training, and higher-risk activities may require a licensed contractor. Do not assume that the word “plasterer” establishes those capabilities.'],
 ['Ask about the proposed method','Once the material and condition are understood, ask the relevant professionals to explain the options, risks and scope. What is staying in place? What would be disturbed? Who is responsible for the assessment and any specialist work?','There is deliberately no DIY removal method here. The right route depends on the actual material, its condition and the proposed work.'],
 ['What to send when enquiring','A wide photograph taken without disturbing the ceiling, the property’s approximate age if known, any existing survey information and the finish you want are useful starting points. Mention damage, leaks or previous repairs.','Lyons Interiors can discuss the plastering enquiry, but this website does not offer asbestos testing or removal. Any specialist requirement must be dealt with through an appropriate provider.']
 ],takeaway:'Identify and assess first. Decide on a smooth finish second. Never disturb a suspect coating for the sake of a quote.',refs:['asbestos'],related:['what-to-send-for-a-quote','skimming-vs-replastering']},
 {slug:'patch-repair-or-reskim',category:'Comparisons & Options',title:'Patch repair or full-wall skim: which is worth paying for?',excerpt:'When a targeted repair makes sense, when a wider finish may help and how to compare the options.',image:'kc07',answer:'A local repair can be the sensible choice for isolated damage on an otherwise suitable wall. A wider skim may be worth discussing where repairs are extensive or you want a consistent finish across the whole surface.',sections:[
 ['Start with the outcome, not the biggest job','Are you making good after electrical work, repairing one damaged area or preparing a whole room for redecoration? These are different objectives. Show the plasterer the complete wall, not only the damaged patch, and explain how you intend to decorate it.'],
 ['Discuss a local repair when the problem is limited','Where the surrounding surface is suitable and the damaged area is isolated, ask what a targeted repair would involve. Clarify how the repair will meet the existing surface and what preparation the decorator will need to allow for afterwards.','Do not assume a small patch automatically means a negligible visit: preparation, access, materials and finishing still need consideration. Ask for the scope rather than guessing from the hole’s size.'],
 ['Discuss a wider finish when the wall has become patchwork','Several repairs, repeated alterations or widespread cosmetic unevenness may make a full-wall finish a more useful option. The condition of the background still needs assessing. A skim is not a remedy for a loose wall or an unresolved moisture problem.','Compare the total work needed to reach your desired decorating outcome, not only the plastering line item in isolation.'],
 ['Ask for the trade-off in plain English','Request an explanation of the smaller and larger options where both are appropriate. What finish should you expect? What preparation remains for the decorator? Which surrounding areas are included?','Neither option should be sold as universally correct. The sensible choice reflects the wall, the cause of the damage, your expectations and the agreed budget.']
 ],takeaway:'Pay for the appropriate scope. Ask why the recommended repair suits the whole wall, not just the visible damage.',related:['plastering-cost-leeds','skimming-vs-replastering']},
 {slug:'choosing-a-plasterer',category:'Choosing a Plasterer',title:'How to choose a plasterer in Leeds without guessing',excerpt:'A practical way to assess the work, compare quotes and avoid being swayed by a glossy website alone.',image:'kc08',answer:'Look for relevant examples of actual work, clear communication and a written scope you understand. Check reviews at their original source and judge the proposed approach as well as the price.',sections:[
 ['Ask to see a job like yours','An impressive finished room is not the same thing as evidence of the plastering beneath it. Ask for examples that show the surfaces and the type of work you need. A whole-house refurbishment, ceiling repair and single-wall skim each raise different questions.','On this site, interior photography is labelled as inspiration. For Lyons Interiors’ own project updates, use the linked Instagram profile and discuss a relevant example directly.'],
 ['Read the detail in reviews','Use the original review platform where possible. Look for what customers actually describe: communication, the agreed work, treatment of the property and how questions were handled. Consider the recency and relevance of the feedback rather than relying on a star graphic.','A review badge should never stand in for checking the source yourself. Ask about experiences relevant to the scale and type of work you are considering.'],
 ['Compare written scopes','A useful quote identifies the surfaces, preparation, finishing, exclusions and payment arrangements. Ask how additional work will be raised and agreed. Discuss the property’s current condition honestly, including any suspected moisture or movement.','For project-specific insurance, qualifications or documentation, request evidence directly. Do not assume these details from branding, a directory listing or an attractive photograph.'],
 ['Notice how the conversation feels','You should be able to ask basic questions without being rushed. A suitable contractor should be able to explain the proposal and identify things that need further assessment. Be cautious about certainty where nobody has considered the background or the project constraints.','Choose the combination of appropriate scope, credible evidence and communication that makes sense for your job—not a claim to be the best plasterer in every situation.']
 ],takeaway:'The right questions beat a polished sales pitch. Ask for evidence, scope and a sensible explanation.',related:['plastering-cost-leeds','what-to-send-for-a-quote']},
 {slug:'planning-your-plastering-project',category:'Planning & Aftercare',title:'Where does plastering fit in your renovation?',excerpt:'Coordinate preparation, other trades, drying and decorating without relying on an unrealistic finish date.',image:'kc09',answer:'Treat preparation, plastering, drying and decorating as separate stages. Agree the sequence with the people doing the work before booking everyone against the same deadline.',sections:[
 ['Work backwards from the room you want','Write down the final changes: new lighting, radiators, sockets, fitted furniture, flooring or decoration. Bring that list to the consultation. It is easier to discuss the right order when everyone understands the complete plan.','Avoid treating plastering as a task that can be inserted anywhere. Changes to walls and services may need completing or coordinating before the final surface is finished.'],
 ['Make the handovers clear','Ask each trade what must be ready before they arrive and what they will leave for the next person. Who is removing fittings? Who is making good? Who checks that services are in their final positions?','Record these responsibilities. A simple shared list of rooms and tasks can prevent the same work being priced twice—or not included by anyone.'],
 ['Allow a separate decorating stage','The end of plastering is not automatically the start of painting. Discuss drying and product-specific preparation with both the plasterer and decorator. Keep the schedule flexible enough for the actual condition of the surfaces.','If you are working to a move-in or business-opening date, explain that at the enquiry stage. Do not treat an unconfirmed date as a guarantee.'],
 ['Decide how the property will be used','For occupied homes, discuss essential access routes and which rooms need to remain usable. For multi-room projects, ask whether staging is appropriate and how it affects the scope and programme.','Before accepting a quote, confirm the proposed sequence, exclusions and how changes will be agreed. A clear plan is more useful than an optimistic promise that everything will somehow fit.']
 ],takeaway:'Plan the handovers as carefully as the finish. Your plasterer needs to know what happens before and after the work.',related:['when-can-you-paint-new-plaster','how-messy-is-plastering']},
 {slug:'commercial-plastering-checklist',category:'Planning & Aftercare',title:'A commercial plastering enquiry checklist',excerpt:'What property owners and contractors should clarify before an interior finishing project is priced.',image:'kc10',answer:'A useful commercial brief explains the surfaces, specification, access, occupancy and programme. Identify any specialist performance or site requirements at the outset.',sections:[
 ['Describe the premises and the scope','State how the space is used, whether it remains occupied and which rooms or areas are involved. Include plans where available, approximate areas and photographs of the existing condition. Distinguish walls, ceilings and local repairs.','Say whether you are the owner, tenant, main contractor or another project contact, and identify who approves changes.'],
 ['Bring the specification into the conversation','If your project has a specified system or performance requirement, provide it before asking for a price. Fire and acoustic performance depend on the relevant construction details; they should never be inferred merely from a newly plastered appearance.','Agree who is responsible for the specification, any supporting documentation and inspection. Do not assume that a general finishing quote includes specialist design or certification.'],
 ['Explain site access and occupancy','Mention operating hours, deliveries, lifts, loading, shared routes and any limits on noisy or disruptive work. Tell the contractor about induction requirements and the arrangements for welfare and storage.','Out-of-hours availability and phased work need a specific agreement. They should not be assumed from a general commercial-services page.'],
 ['Confirm programme and acceptance','Explain the required sequence with other trades and any intended opening or handover date. Ask for a proposed programme subject to assessing the actual work. Define the surfaces to be handed over and how outstanding items will be recorded.','Check the total, VAT treatment, payment stages and the process for variations. A clear brief is the best starting point for comparing like-for-like proposals.']
 ],takeaway:'Share the constraints as well as the drawings. A commercial quote is only as clear as the brief behind it.',related:['planning-your-plastering-project','plastering-cost-leeds']},
 {slug:'what-to-send-for-a-quote',category:'Costs & Pricing',title:'What should you send a plasterer for an accurate quote?',excerpt:'Five useful pieces of information that make the first conversation much more productive.',image:'kc11',answer:'Send your postcode, the surfaces involved, wide photographs, visible problem details and your timing. An initial conversation may still need a site visit before the scope and price can be confirmed.',sections:[
 ['1. The location and type of property','A postcode helps establish the location. Say whether the job is in a house, flat or commercial premises. Mention stairs, parking restrictions and anything unusual about access. You do not need to include personal documents or sensitive information in a first message.'],
 ['2. The surfaces you want finished','Explain whether the enquiry concerns one wall, every wall, a ceiling or several rooms. Rough measurements are useful when available; label them clearly rather than supplying one unexplained number. Tell us what finish you want and what is currently on the surface.'],
 ['3. Photographs with context','Take a wide view of each room or surface, then closer photographs of visible damage. Include corners and the surrounding area where relevant. A close-up alone can make it difficult to understand the scale.','Do not disturb coatings, open up walls or climb unsafely to obtain a photograph. Existing survey information can be shared where it is relevant and you are authorised to share it.'],
 ['4. Problems and other planned work','Mention leaks, loose surfaces, recurring cracks or previous repairs honestly. Explain whether electrical work, plumbing or fitted furniture is still planned. These details help avoid discussing a cosmetic finish before the underlying situation is understood.'],
 ['5. Your timing and next step','Say whether you are gathering ideas, ready to arrange a visit or working towards a target date. An initial target is not a confirmed booking.','Use our enquiry builder to organise the details into a message. You choose whether to send it by text or WhatsApp, or copy it for another conversation. Nothing is sent automatically.']
 ],takeaway:'Context saves back-and-forth. A clear brief is more useful than asking for “a price to do a room”.',related:['plastering-cost-leeds','prepare-a-room-for-plastering']},
 {slug:'is-cheapest-plastering-quote-best',category:'Costs & Pricing',title:'Is the cheapest plastering quote the best value?',excerpt:'Sometimes a smaller quote is right. Sometimes you are comparing two completely different jobs.',image:'kc12',answer:'The cheapest quote may be the best choice when it covers an appropriate scope and clear expectations. It is not automatically better—or worse—because of the total alone.',sections:[
 ['A lower price can have a sensible explanation','A contractor might be quoting a targeted repair while another has allowed for an entire wall. One proposal may assume you clear the room or arrange preparatory work separately. A simpler approach could be right for your circumstances.','Ask what explains the difference. Do not assume every higher price means better workmanship, or that a low quote must be cutting corners.'],
 ['Find the missing assumptions','Put the quotes side by side. Are the same surfaces included? Is the existing background being treated in the same way? Who supplies materials, protects the route and deals with waste? Is VAT applicable and included?','Check whether the decorator will need to do additional preparation after each proposed option. A saving in one stage may create work elsewhere, but that needs discussing rather than guessing.'],
 ['Know what happens when something changes','Existing surfaces can require further assessment. Ask how unexpected conditions will be explained, priced and authorised. Avoid arrangements that leave you uncertain about whether additional work can proceed without your agreement.','Also clarify payment timing and what marks completion of the agreed work. Both sides benefit from a shared understanding.'],
 ['Choose for your actual goal','For a limited repair, a modest, well-scoped proposal may be entirely appropriate. For a more extensive refurbishment, you may place greater weight on coordination, continuity of finish and the agreed programme.','The purpose of comparing quotes is not to justify the most expensive option. It is to understand what each buys and choose the right fit for your room, expectations and budget.']
 ],takeaway:'Good value is an appropriate job, clearly priced. Ask what the difference buys before deciding.',related:['plastering-cost-leeds','choosing-a-plasterer']},
{"slug":"cost-to-skim-a-room-leeds","category":"Costs & Pricing","title":"How much does it cost to skim a room in Leeds?","excerpt":"Why room prices vary, what a quote should describe and the details worth sending before asking for a figure.","image":'kc13',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"There is no reliable one-price-fits-all figure for skimming a room. The amount of surface, ceiling height, condition of the background, preparation, access and whether the ceiling is included all change the job. A useful quote describes the surfaces and preparation as well as the total.","sections":[["Start with surfaces, not floor area","A small room can have a lot of plastering if it has high ceilings, alcoves, chimney breasts or several reveals. A larger room with straightforward, sound walls can be simpler. When asking for a quote, say whether you mean one wall, all walls, the ceiling, or the complete room.","Photographs help, but include rough dimensions where you can. A wide photograph plus a few close-ups of damaged areas is much more useful than a single close-up with no sense of scale."],["Preparation is often where quotes differ","Two plasterers can appear to quote for the same room while allowing for different preparation. One may have included removal of loose material, repairs and bonding preparation; another may be pricing only the finishing skim over a background assumed to be sound.","Ask what happens before the finish coat goes on. That question often explains more of the price difference than the room size itself."],["Access and room condition matter","A clear room with straightforward access is different from a furnished room where large items need protecting or working around. Stairs, parking, top-floor flats and restricted working hours can also affect the practical arrangement.","Do not empty the room or strip surfaces simply because you think it will reduce the price. Agree what preparation is actually useful first."],["How to ask for a sensible figure","Send the postcode, room dimensions, which surfaces are included, several photographs and any known issues such as loose plaster, leaks or recurring cracks. Say whether other trades are still working in the room.","The result should be a quote for an identified scope, not just a number labelled 'one room'. That makes it easier to compare like with like."]],"takeaway":"For a room skim, compare the scope behind the number. The best first step is a clear brief with photographs and the surfaces listed.","related":["plastering-cost-leeds","what-to-send-for-a-quote","is-cheapest-plastering-quote-best"]},
{"slug":"ceiling-plastering-cost-leeds","category":"Costs & Pricing","title":"How much does ceiling plastering cost in Leeds?","excerpt":"The size is only part of the price. Existing coatings, access, repairs and the condition above your head can matter just as much.","image":'kc14',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Ceiling plastering is priced around the actual ceiling and preparation required. Area, height, access, existing texture, cracks, water damage and whether overboarding or repair work is needed can all change the scope.","sections":[["Why ceiling prices are not just square metres","A flat, sound ceiling at standard height is a different job from a high stairwell, a ceiling with several damaged sections or one with a heavy textured coating. Lighting, coving, beams and awkward edges can add detail even when the overall area is modest."],["Existing texture can change the conversation","If a ceiling has a textured decorative coating, do not scrape or sand it to help with a quote. Some older coatings can contain asbestos. The material and proposed work need to be considered before any disturbance is planned.","Send a whole-ceiling photograph and close-ups, but leave the coating intact until the appropriate next step is clear."],["Repairs, overboarding and skimming are different scopes","A ceiling may need only suitable preparation and a finish, or the condition may lead to a discussion about local repair or a new board lining. These are not interchangeable prices. Ask the plasterer what is being retained, what is being repaired and what the finished surface will be built on."],["What a good quote should tell you","The quote should identify the ceiling or rooms included, the preparation allowed for, the proposed approach, protection and any exclusions. If light fittings or electrical work need attention, establish who is responsible before the plastering date."]],"takeaway":"A useful ceiling quote explains the background and preparation, not just the room dimensions.","refs":["asbestos"],"related":["textured-ceilings-and-asbestos","what-to-send-for-a-quote","overboard-or-skim-ceiling"]},
{"slug":"what-affects-plastering-quote","category":"Costs & Pricing","title":"What affects the price of a plastering quote?","excerpt":"Seven practical reasons two apparently similar plastering jobs can come back at very different prices.","image":'kc15',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Plastering prices change with scope, condition, preparation, access, detail, sequencing and risk. The headline area matters, but it rarely explains the whole quote.","sections":[["1. The amount of surface","Walls, ceilings, reveals, alcoves and returns all add working area. Floor area alone is a poor shortcut because two rooms with the same footprint can have very different surfaces."],["2. The condition underneath","A sound background suitable for a finishing skim is different from loose plaster, damaged board, heavy patching or moisture-affected material. Preparation that creates a sound background is part of the job, even though it disappears beneath the finish."],["3. Height, detail and access","Tall ceilings, stairwells, tight spaces and detailed edges change how the work can be carried out. Parking, loading and access through occupied areas can also affect the practical setup."],["4. What happens around the plastering","Other trades, fitted kitchens, radiators, electrics and decorating all influence sequencing. Ask whether protection, waste arrangements and making good are included. The clearest quote is the one where you can see exactly what the total covers."]],"takeaway":"When quotes differ, compare the surfaces, preparation and exclusions before comparing the totals.","related":["plastering-cost-leeds","is-cheapest-plastering-quote-best","what-should-plastering-quote-include"]},
{"slug":"plasterer-day-rate-vs-fixed-price","category":"Costs & Pricing","title":"Plasterer day rate or fixed price: which should you ask for?","excerpt":"A day rate can suit uncertain work; a fixed price can suit a defined scope. The important bit is knowing what each arrangement actually covers.","image":'kc16',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Neither a day rate nor a fixed price is automatically better. A fixed project price is easier to compare when the scope is clear. Day-rate work can be reasonable where the extent cannot be established in advance, but you need a clear basis for materials, hours and approval of extra work.","sections":[["When a fixed price is useful","If the surfaces and preparation can be assessed in advance, a fixed quote gives both sides a defined scope and total. Ask for the included rooms, surfaces, preparation and exclusions to be written down rather than relying on a one-line figure."],["When a day rate can make sense","Investigative or piecemeal repair work can contain genuine uncertainty. In that situation, a day rate may avoid pretending the contractor knows the exact extent before opening up failed areas. The arrangement should still explain working hours, materials and what needs your approval."],["Do not compare a day rate with a project price as if they are the same","A lower-looking daily figure tells you little if you do not know how many days, how many people or what materials sit outside it. Likewise, a project total is only comparable if the underlying scope matches."],["Ask how changes will be handled","Whichever model is used, agree what happens if the background is worse than expected. You should understand how additional work is explained, authorised and priced before it proceeds."]],"takeaway":"Choose the pricing method that makes the scope transparent. Clarity matters more than the label 'day rate' or 'fixed price'.","related":["is-cheapest-plastering-quote-best","what-should-plastering-quote-include","choosing-a-plasterer"]},
{"slug":"hidden-costs-plastering-quote","category":"Costs & Pricing","title":"What 'hidden extras' should you check in a plastering quote?","excerpt":"Preparation, protection, waste, access and follow-on work are the usual places where an unclear quote becomes an expensive surprise.","image":'kc17',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"The best way to avoid surprise costs is to make the scope explicit. Ask what preparation, materials, protection, waste arrangements and making good are included, and what would count as additional work.","sections":[["Preparation before the skim","Removing loose material, repairing damaged areas, controlling suction or creating a suitable key may be needed before finishing plaster. Do not assume 'skim room' means every form of preparation is included."],["Protection and moving items","Clarify whether the room needs to be empty, who moves large furniture and what protection is supplied for floors and access routes. If something cannot be moved, say so before the quote is finalised."],["Other trades and fittings","Radiators, sockets, lighting, fitted units and plumbing can affect access. Plastering quotes do not automatically include electrical, plumbing, carpentry or decorating work. Confirm the hand-off between trades."],["Waste, VAT and additional work","Check how waste from the agreed work is handled, whether VAT applies, and how extra work would be approved if hidden damage is discovered. A good quote does not need to predict every surprise, but it should make the process for surprises clear."]],"takeaway":"Ask what is included, what is excluded and what could legitimately change the price. That is how 'extras' stop being hidden.","related":["plastering-cost-leeds","what-should-plastering-quote-include","is-cheapest-plastering-quote-best"]},
{"slug":"does-empty-room-make-plastering-cheaper","category":"Costs & Pricing","title":"Will emptying a room make plastering cheaper?","excerpt":"Sometimes clearer access reduces handling and protection. But moving everything yourself only helps if it matches the plasterer’s plan.","image":'kc18',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"A clear room can make access and protection simpler, but it does not automatically reduce the quote. Ask what the plasterer needs moved before you dismantle furniture or start stripping surfaces.","sections":[["Clear access is useful","Small belongings, fragile items and clutter should normally be removed from the agreed working area. That gives safer access and reduces the amount of personal property that needs protecting."],["Large furniture needs a plan","A wardrobe that cannot leave the room may be workable in one position and completely obstructive in another. Do not assume pushing everything into the centre is always the right answer, especially when ceilings are included."],["Do not create extra work trying to save money","Unplanned wallpaper stripping, removing trims or disturbing old coatings can expose additional problems. If a surface could contain asbestos or another unknown material, disturbance can also create a safety issue. Ask first."],["Price depends on more than furniture","Background condition, repairs, size and access usually matter more than whether a room is perfectly empty. Tell the plasterer what can and cannot be moved so the quote reflects the real working arrangement."]],"takeaway":"Clear what is genuinely helpful, not everything you can physically remove. Agree the room-preparation plan before the start date.","related":["prepare-a-room-for-plastering","how-messy-is-plastering","plastering-cost-leeds"]},
{"slug":"plaster-repair-cost-vs-reskim","category":"Costs & Pricing","title":"Is a plaster repair cheaper than reskimming the whole wall?","excerpt":"A smaller repair can cost less, but the cheapest square metre is not always the cleanest overall result.","image":'kc19',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"A local repair can be the economical choice where damage is genuinely limited and the surrounding plaster is sound. A wider skim can make more sense when many repairs interrupt the same wall or the existing finish is poor across a large area.","sections":[["Start with the cause of the damage","A one-off impact hole is a different situation from damage caused by ongoing damp or movement. Do not price the cosmetic repair before understanding whether the cause is still active."],["The surrounding surface matters","A neat local patch can work well when the rest of the wall is stable and the decorating plan can accommodate it. If the surface is covered in old patches, ridges or widespread defects, the labour involved in blending many small repairs can change the comparison."],["Ask what finish you are buying","Two quotes may use different definitions of 'repair'. Ask how far the damaged material is being removed, how the repair is prepared and whether the rest of the wall is included in the finish."],["Choose the smallest appropriate scope","There is no prize for reskimming more wall than necessary. Equally, repeatedly patching a surface that is failing more widely can be false economy. The correct decision depends on the actual background and the finish you want."]],"takeaway":"Compare the repair and full-wall options by outcome, preparation and cause—not just by the smallest initial price.","related":["patch-repair-or-reskim","repair-cracks-or-reskim-wall","is-cheapest-plastering-quote-best"]},
{"slug":"is-plastering-worth-it-before-selling","category":"Costs & Pricing","title":"Is it worth replastering before selling a house?","excerpt":"Fresh plaster can improve presentation, but spending on surfaces only makes sense when it addresses a visible problem buyers would otherwise notice.","image":'kc20',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Replastering before a sale can be worthwhile when damaged or very uneven surfaces are obvious, but it is not automatically a good return on every room. Consider the condition, decorating required afterwards, your sale timetable and whether an underlying issue needs attention first.","sections":[["Fix problems before cosmetics","If staining, movement or loose plaster points to an unresolved issue, simply making the wall look smoother is the wrong order. Deal with the cause first and keep relevant records where appropriate."],["Think about the whole finish","New plaster still needs suitable drying time and decoration. If your sale timetable is tight, include those stages rather than treating the plasterer’s finish date as the end of the work."],["Prioritise what buyers will actually see","A badly damaged hallway or main living space may affect first impressions more than a minor defect in a secondary room. That does not mean every imperfection needs a full reskim."],["Avoid pretending cosmetic work changes the property itself","Plastering can improve surfaces and presentation. It does not increase room size, fix structural defects or solve damp. Keep the decision proportionate to the problem you are trying to solve."]],"takeaway":"Before selling, plaster the surfaces that genuinely need it and allow time for drying and decorating. Do not use a skim to hide an unresolved defect.","related":["patch-repair-or-reskim","planning-your-plastering-project","when-can-you-paint-new-plaster"]},
{"slug":"cracks-in-new-plaster","category":"Problems & Risks","title":"Why has my new plaster cracked?","excerpt":"Some cracks are linked to drying or movement; others point to what is happening underneath. The pattern and whether it changes matter.","image":'kc21',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"A crack in new plaster can have several causes, including movement in the background, joints, shrinkage or an issue with the substrate. A photograph can show the pattern, but diagnosis depends on what the wall is built from and whether the crack is stable or changing.","sections":[["Look at the pattern, not just the width","A fine line following a board joint tells a different story from a crack running diagonally from a doorway or one that keeps reopening. Note when it appeared and whether it has changed rather than repeatedly filling it without understanding the pattern."],["New plaster still depends on the background","A finish coat is not independent of the wall behind it. Movement, weak backgrounds or poorly resolved junctions can show through the surface. The right repair may involve more than filling the visible line."],["Know when to get a wider building assessment","If cracks are growing, repeatedly returning, accompanied by sticking doors or other signs of movement, or you are concerned about the building rather than only the finish, seek an appropriately competent building professional. A plastering article cannot determine structural significance."],["For stable cosmetic cracks, plan the repair around decoration","Once the cause and stability are understood, a local repair may be possible. Avoid rushing to repaint before the repair and surface are ready, otherwise the same line can reappear through a fresh finish."]],"takeaway":"Track the crack before hiding it. Stable cosmetic damage and continuing building movement are different problems and need different responses.","related":["repair-cracks-or-reskim-wall","patch-repair-or-reskim","plaster-cracking-around-windows-doors"]},
{"slug":"plaster-cracking-around-windows-doors","category":"Problems & Risks","title":"Why does plaster crack around windows and doors?","excerpt":"Openings concentrate junctions, different materials and movement. That makes the pattern around corners worth looking at before simply filling it.","image":'kc22',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Cracks around windows and doors can be associated with junctions, local movement or previous repairs. The useful next step is to understand whether the crack is stable and cosmetic or part of continuing movement.","sections":[["Corners are busy parts of a wall","Openings bring together reveals, lintels, frames and wall surfaces. Different materials and junctions can move differently, so cracks often appear at corners or along previous repair lines."],["Check for a recurring pattern","If the same crack returns after filling, photograph it and note whether it changes with time. A repeated repair that fails in exactly the same place is useful information for whoever assesses the wall."],["Separate finishing issues from building issues","A plasterer can assess the condition of the finish and background they are working on. If cracking is progressive or accompanied by wider signs of movement, an appropriate building assessment may be needed before cosmetic work."],["Repair to the cause, not just the line","Once the situation is understood, a local repair may be enough. In other cases a wider area needs preparing so the new finish has a suitable background. The visible crack is only the starting point."]],"takeaway":"Recurring cracks around openings deserve context. Record the pattern and resolve any wider movement concern before paying to hide it again.","related":["cracks-in-new-plaster","repair-cracks-or-reskim-wall","patch-repair-or-reskim"]},
{"slug":"blown-plaster-how-to-tell","category":"Problems & Risks","title":"What is blown plaster and how can you tell?","excerpt":"Hollow sounds and local movement can suggest plaster has lost its bond, but the amount of failure matters before deciding what to remove.","image":'kc23',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"'Blown plaster' is a common description for plaster that has lost adhesion to the background. Hollow-sounding or visibly loose areas can be clues, but the extent needs assessing before deciding between a local repair and more extensive work.","sections":[["What people mean by blown plaster","The phrase usually describes a layer that is no longer securely bonded to what is behind it. You may notice movement, cracking, bulging or a different sound when an experienced person checks the surface."],["Do not turn a small check into demolition","Knocking, drilling or pulling at a suspect wall can make loose areas fail further. If the property is older or unknown materials may be present, avoid unnecessary disturbance and let the person assessing the job decide what needs opening up."],["The boundary of the problem matters","One isolated failed patch is different from a wall where large areas are unsound. A plasterer may need to identify where the background becomes firm before recommending a repair."],["Ask what will remain under the new finish","If loose material is simply covered, the new surface is still relying on the failed layer underneath. A useful quote should explain what unsound material is removed or stabilised and what background the new work will rely on."]],"takeaway":"The important question is not 'is it blown?' but 'how much of the background is still sound?'.","related":["hollow-sounding-plaster","patch-repair-or-reskim","skimming-vs-replastering"]},
{"slug":"damp-wall-before-plastering","category":"Problems & Risks","title":"What should happen to a damp wall before plastering?","excerpt":"The moisture source comes first. A smooth new finish is not a substitute for fixing why the wall is damp.","image":'kc24',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"A damp wall should not simply be covered because the finish looks poor. Identify and resolve the moisture source, allow the appropriate remedial process to take place, then choose a plastering or lining system suitable for the background.","sections":[["Find the cause before choosing the finish","Moisture can come from leaks, rainwater defects, condensation, plumbing or other building issues. The correct remedy depends on the source, so 'plaster over it' is not a diagnosis."],["Salt and contamination can affect the background","British Gypsum notes that prolonged damp or condensation can be associated with salt contamination and that the underlying cause should be resolved before the finishing approach is chosen. That is one reason a damp wall may need more than an ordinary skim."],["Water-damaged board may need replacement","Where plasterboard has been directly water damaged, bowing, mould growth or paper delamination are warning signs that replacement may be appropriate. The condition and product system need to be considered rather than assuming drying alone restores performance."],["Build the programme around the remedial work","Do not book plastering solely to cover a stain before the source has been repaired. Share what caused the damp, what work has been completed and any specialist advice you have received so the next stage can be planned around a dry, suitable background."]],"takeaway":"Fix the moisture problem first, then specify the wall finish. Cosmetic plastering should be the last step in the diagnosis, not the first.","refs":["gypsumDamp","gypsumWater"],"related":["can-you-plaster-over-damp","old-plaster-falling-off-what-next","planning-your-plastering-project"]},
{"slug":"can-you-plaster-over-damp","category":"Problems & Risks","title":"Can you plaster over a damp wall?","excerpt":"A new skim can hide the mark for a while, but it does not fix the source of moisture underneath.","image":'kc25',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Plastering over an actively damp wall is not a sensible way to solve the problem. The source of moisture needs identifying and dealing with first, then the wall can be assessed for the appropriate repair, lining or plaster system.","sections":[["Damp is a cause problem before it is a finish problem","A stained or cold-feeling wall may look like a decorating issue, but the moisture could be coming from several sources. Covering it before the cause is understood can leave the new finish relying on an unsuitable background."],["Moisture can change the plastering specification","Prolonged damp can be associated with salts, damaged backgrounds and mould. British Gypsum advises resolving the cause of salt contamination before deciding on the wall-lining or plaster approach.","That means a normal finishing skim is not automatically the next step once you have seen a damp patch."],["Do not use drying time as proof the problem has gone","A wall can look drier after a spell of warm weather while the source remains. If the moisture comes back, the new decoration and plaster can be affected again. Keep records of repairs and ask what evidence is being used to decide the background is ready."],["Tell the plasterer what has already happened","Share the history: where the damp appeared, what caused it if known, what remedial work has been done and whether any specialist advice exists. That is much more useful than simply asking for a reskim."]],"takeaway":"Treat damp as a building issue first and a plastering issue second. Fix the source before paying to cover the symptom.","refs":["gypsumDamp"],"related":["damp-wall-before-plastering","old-plaster-falling-off-what-next","skimming-vs-replastering"]},
{"slug":"hollow-sounding-plaster","category":"Problems & Risks","title":"Why does my plaster sound hollow when tapped?","excerpt":"A hollow sound can point to poor bond or a void, but it does not tell you how much of the wall needs replacing.","image":'kc26',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"A hollow sound can indicate that plaster is not fully bonded to the background, but tapping alone does not define the repair. The important questions are whether the surface moves, cracks or bulges, how large the affected area is and whether the surrounding background is sound.","sections":[["A sound is only one clue","Different wall build-ups can sound different even when they are serviceable. Treat a hollow note as information to investigate, not a diagnosis that automatically means the whole wall must come off."],["Look for other signs","Movement under gentle pressure, bulging, repeated cracking or edges that have already broken away make the condition more significant. Photograph the area and mark where the symptoms change rather than repeatedly knocking the wall."],["The repair depends on the boundary","If a small section has lost bond but the surrounding material is firm, a local repair may be realistic. Widespread loss of adhesion changes the discussion because any new finish still depends on what remains underneath."],["Ask what the new work will be bonded to","A good repair proposal should explain what loose material is being removed, how the background will be prepared and where sound material begins. That is more useful than a generic promise to 'skim over it'."]],"takeaway":"A hollow sound is a reason to assess the background, not a reason to strip the entire room without evidence.","related":["blown-plaster-how-to-tell","patch-repair-or-reskim","old-plaster-falling-off-what-next"]},
{"slug":"paint-peeling-after-plastering","category":"Problems & Risks","title":"Why is paint peeling off new plaster?","excerpt":"Moisture, preparation and the paint system all matter. The right fix starts by working out which layer has actually failed.","image":'kc27',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Peeling paint over new plaster can be linked to painting before the surface is ready, unsuitable preparation or a problem at the plaster/background interface. First establish whether only the paint is peeling or the plaster itself is losing adhesion.","sections":[["Work out which layer is failing","If only the paint film is lifting, the investigation is different from plaster coming away with it. Take close photographs and note whether the plaster beneath is firm, powdery, damp or loose."],["Drying and paint preparation matter","British Gypsum identifies residual moisture and premature painting among possible contributors to delamination problems. Follow the plaster and paint manufacturers’ instructions rather than relying on a universal number of days or a copied dilution recipe."],["Do not keep adding more paint","Extra coats can hide the extent temporarily but make diagnosis harder. Remove only what is appropriate once you understand the failure and avoid aggressive scraping where the background or coating is uncertain."],["Bring the plasterer and decorator information together","Tell each trade what products and preparation were used. A failure at the interface can sit between stages, so product details and timing are useful evidence rather than blame."]],"takeaway":"Before repainting, identify whether the paint, plaster or background has failed. The repair depends on the layer that actually lost adhesion.","refs":["gypsum"],"related":["when-can-you-paint-new-plaster","decorate-after-plastering-timeline","old-plaster-falling-off-what-next"]},
{"slug":"old-plaster-falling-off-what-next","category":"Problems & Risks","title":"Old plaster is falling off the wall: what should you do next?","excerpt":"Stop treating it as a filling job. Loose plaster needs the surrounding background and the cause of failure checked first.","image":'kc28',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"When old plaster is actively falling away, make the area safe and assess why it has failed before deciding on the finish. Loose material, damp, previous water damage or a weak background can all change the repair.","sections":[["Keep people away from loose overhead or unstable areas","If material is falling from a ceiling or high wall, do not stand beneath it to investigate. A competent person may need to make the area safe before cosmetic repair is discussed."],["Look for the reason it became loose","Age alone is not a complete diagnosis. Check for leaks, damp history, repeated cracking, previous patching or impact damage. If moisture is involved, deal with the source before replastering."],["Water damage can change what stays","British Gypsum advises replacement of water-damaged plasterboard where performance has been compromised, particularly where boards are bowed, mouldy or delaminating. Existing plaster affected by water also needs suitable assessment rather than being assumed sound once dry."],["Expect the scope to become clearer after assessment","A small failed patch may stop at firm surrounding material. Another wall may prove unsound more widely. Ask for the proposed boundary of removal and what will form the background for the new finish."]],"takeaway":"Falling plaster is a background problem before it is a finishing problem. Make it safe, find the cause and repair back to sound material.","refs":["gypsumWater"],"related":["blown-plaster-how-to-tell","damp-wall-before-plastering","skimming-vs-replastering"]},
{"slug":"ceiling-cracks-when-to-worry","category":"Problems & Risks","title":"Cracks in a ceiling: when is it more than a plastering job?","excerpt":"Fine stable cracks and changing movement are not the same thing. Know when to stop planning a cosmetic repair and get the building assessed.","image":'kc29',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"A ceiling crack is not automatically structural, but a plasterer cannot determine every cause from appearance alone. If cracking is changing, widespread, associated with sagging, water damage or other building movement, get an appropriate assessment before cosmetic plastering.","sections":[["Start with what has changed","Note when the crack appeared, whether it is growing and whether anything else changed at the same time. A long-standing hairline crack that has not moved is different from a fresh crack accompanied by sagging or repeated reopening."],["Water changes the priority","Staining, a recent leak or a soft ceiling needs the water source dealt with before finishing work. Water-damaged board may require replacement depending on condition rather than simply drying and skimming."],["Movement signs deserve wider assessment","If you see multiple changing cracks, distortion, doors beginning to stick or other movement around the property, speak to an appropriately competent building professional. Do not use plastering as a way to cover evidence you still need to understand."],["Once the background is understood, the finish can be planned","Stable cosmetic cracking may be repairable locally. Widespread or repeatedly failing surfaces may need a broader approach. The plastering specification should follow the diagnosis, not replace it."]],"takeaway":"If the ceiling is changing, sagging or water-damaged, pause the cosmetic work. Understand the building issue first.","refs":["gypsumWater"],"related":["cracks-in-new-plaster","plaster-cracking-around-windows-doors","overboard-or-skim-ceiling"]},
{"slug":"overboard-or-skim-ceiling","category":"Comparisons & Options","title":"Should you overboard a ceiling or skim the existing one?","excerpt":"A skim keeps the existing background. Overboarding creates a new board surface. The right choice depends on what is already overhead.","image":'kc30',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Skimming can suit an existing ceiling that is sound and properly prepared. Overboarding may be considered where the existing surface is unsuitable for a direct finish or where a new board lining is part of the chosen system. The background and any textured coating must be assessed first.","sections":[["What a skim relies on","A skim is a finishing layer. It still depends on the ceiling beneath it being suitable, stable and correctly prepared. It is not a structural fix for a sagging or failing ceiling."],["What overboarding changes","Overboarding creates a new plasterboard face fixed as part of a new lining. That can change the build-up, edges, fittings and detailing, so it is more than simply 'a thicker skim'."],["Textured ceilings need an asbestos check where relevant","Older textured decorative coatings can contain asbestos. Do not scrape or drill a suspect coating to test how well it is stuck. The proposed method needs to take the material and safe working requirements into account."],["Ask for the reason behind the recommendation","If one plasterer suggests a skim and another suggests overboarding, ask what each has found in the existing ceiling. The useful comparison is the condition they are designing around, not just the price difference."]],"takeaway":"Skim when the existing ceiling is a suitable background; consider a new board lining when it is not. Let the condition drive the method.","refs":["asbestos"],"related":["textured-ceilings-and-asbestos","ceiling-plastering-cost-leeds","ceiling-cracks-when-to-worry"]},
{"slug":"plasterboard-or-wet-plaster","category":"Comparisons & Options","title":"Plasterboard or traditional wet plaster: which is right for an interior wall?","excerpt":"Both can produce a finished wall, but they solve different build-up, programme and background problems.","image":'kc31',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Plasterboard lining and wet plaster are different wall systems rather than simply two brands of the same finish. The right choice depends on the existing construction, required build-up, services, performance needs and condition of the background.","sections":[["Plasterboard creates a lining","Board systems can provide a new surface and can be fixed in different ways depending on the wall design. The final build-up may also need to meet acoustic, fire or moisture requirements where those are part of the project."],["Wet plaster works with the masonry or prepared background","Traditional plaster systems rely on a suitable solid background and the correct preparation for suction, bond and thickness. Existing wall condition therefore has a direct effect on what is practical."],["Room size and details can change","Any new lining changes the wall build-up. Around windows, doors, sockets, skirtings and fitted units, even a modest change in thickness can affect details. Discuss these interfaces rather than choosing solely on the headline method."],["Performance requirements are project-specific","For fire, acoustic or moisture performance, use a system supported by the relevant manufacturer evidence and design information. Do not assume that every board or plaster build-up performs the same."]],"takeaway":"Choose the wall system around the existing construction and required performance. 'Board or wet plaster' is a design decision, not just a finish preference.","refs":["gypsum"],"related":["skimming-vs-replastering","planning-your-plastering-project","skim-over-old-plaster"]},
{"slug":"repair-cracks-or-reskim-wall","category":"Comparisons & Options","title":"Should you repair cracks or reskim the whole wall?","excerpt":"One stable crack may need a local repair. A wall full of defects can be a different calculation.","image":'kc32',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Repairing cracks locally can be appropriate when the cause is understood, the cracking is stable and the surrounding finish is sound. A wider reskim can make sense where defects are widespread, but it still will not solve continuing movement.","sections":[["Count defects, but also understand them","A wall with one old stable line is different from a surface covered in recurring cracks. Before discussing how much plaster to apply, establish whether the cracking is cosmetic or still changing."],["A reskim is not a movement cure","A fresh finish can make a wall uniform, but if the background continues to move the crack can return. Do not use a larger cosmetic scope as a substitute for understanding the cause."],["Local repairs have to blend into the existing finish","A small patch can be economical, but the surrounding texture, paint build-up and flatness affect how easily it disappears after decoration. Discuss the final visual expectation as well as the repair itself."],["Choose the smallest scope that gives a durable result","That may be one repair, several repairs and local finishing, or a complete skim of the wall. The decision should follow the condition, not a rule that bigger work is always better."]],"takeaway":"Fix the cause, then choose the smallest repair scope that gives the finish you want.","related":["cracks-in-new-plaster","patch-repair-or-reskim","plaster-repair-cost-vs-reskim"]},
{"slug":"skim-over-painted-walls","category":"Comparisons & Options","title":"Can you skim plaster over painted walls?","excerpt":"Sometimes—but only when the paint and the background are genuinely sound and the surface is prepared for adhesion.","image":'kc33',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"British Gypsum states that plastering onto an existing painted wall is possible only where both the paint and the substrate are in very good condition, with appropriate preparation and a trial area to confirm adhesion.","sections":[["The new plaster is only as secure as the paint underneath","If paint is flaking, poorly bonded or sitting on a weak background, covering it does not remove that weak layer. Preparation starts by deciding what is actually sound enough to remain."],["Cleanliness and adhesion matter","Grease, dust and unstable coatings can interfere with bond. British Gypsum recommends suitable bonding preparation and a trial area when plastering over sound painted surfaces."],["Not every painted wall should be treated the same","Glossy paint, repeated old coatings, unknown primers and patched areas can create different conditions across one wall. A plasterer may need to assess or test rather than apply one preparation blindly across everything."],["Do not scrape unknown old coatings without thinking first","In older properties, be cautious about disturbing materials you have not identified. The useful first step is to show the existing finish and let the proposed preparation follow from what is there."]],"takeaway":"Yes, a painted wall can sometimes be skimmed—but only when the paint and substrate are sound and the surface is prepared for a reliable bond.","refs":["gypsumPainted"],"related":["skim-over-old-plaster","plaster-over-wallpaper","skimming-vs-replastering"]},
{"slug":"plaster-over-wallpaper","category":"Comparisons & Options","title":"Can you plaster over wallpaper?","excerpt":"Wallpaper is not a dependable background for a new plaster finish. The wall underneath needs to be exposed and assessed.","image":'kc34',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Plaster should not be treated as a way to permanently bury wallpaper. The paper, paste and the condition of the wall beneath can interfere with the new finish, so the background needs to be assessed and prepared.","sections":[["The plaster would be relying on the wallpaper bond","Even if the paper looks flat, its adhesive was not designed to carry a plaster finish. Moisture from new work can also affect old paste and paper."],["Removing wallpaper can reveal the real job","Once paper is off, you may find sound plaster, old patches, torn plasterboard paper or loose areas. That information determines whether the wall needs local preparation, repair or a wider skim."],["Do not aggressively strip a wall you have not assessed","Steamers, scraping and soaking can damage weak backgrounds. In older properties or where the wall build-up is uncertain, agree the preparation method rather than attacking the surface simply to save time."],["Tell the plasterer what is currently on the wall","Photographs of the wallpaper and any areas already lifting can help. If removal is your responsibility, make sure the quote says what condition the wall is expected to be in afterwards."]],"takeaway":"Take wallpaper out of the plaster build-up. Expose the real background, then decide what preparation and finish it needs.","related":["skim-over-painted-walls","skim-over-old-plaster","prepare-a-room-for-plastering"]},
{"slug":"skim-over-old-plaster","category":"Comparisons & Options","title":"Can you skim over old plaster?","excerpt":"Often, if the old plaster is sound. The decision turns on adhesion, cracks, damp and what is hiding under the surface.","image":'kc35',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Old plaster can sometimes provide a suitable background for a new skim, but only if it is sufficiently sound and properly prepared. Loose, damp or failing plaster should not simply be hidden beneath a new finish.","sections":[["Age is not the deciding factor","A decades-old wall can be firm and suitable for preparation, while a newer wall can have widespread bond failure. Assess condition rather than assuming 'old' means removal."],["Check for loose areas and recurring problems","Cracks, hollow areas, bulges, damp staining and friable surfaces all change the discussion. A finishing skim cannot make a weak layer underneath mechanically sound."],["Preparation depends on the existing surface","Paint, high or low suction and previous patching affect how the background needs preparing. Manufacturer guidance and trial areas can be appropriate where adhesion is uncertain."],["Ask what stays and what comes off","A useful quote should make clear whether damaged areas are being removed locally, whether the whole wall is retained, and what preparation is planned before the finish coat."]],"takeaway":"Skim old plaster when it is genuinely sound and prepared correctly. Remove or repair what is failing rather than burying it.","refs":["gypsum"],"related":["skimming-vs-replastering","blown-plaster-how-to-tell","skim-over-painted-walls"]},
{"slug":"reskim-whole-room-or-one-wall","category":"Comparisons & Options","title":"Should you reskim the whole room or just one wall?","excerpt":"Matching every wall is not always necessary. The best scope depends on condition, light, existing texture and your decorating plans.","image":'kc36',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"You do not automatically need to reskim every wall because one wall is poor. If the other surfaces are sound and acceptable for the finish you want, a single-wall job can be entirely sensible.","sections":[["Start with what is actually wrong","If one wall has extensive patching or damage but the others are flat and sound, treating only that wall may be proportionate. A full-room skim should solve a full-room problem, not simply make the quote bigger."],["Think about the final decorated room","Strong side light, very smooth paint finishes and differences in existing texture can make repaired and untouched surfaces read differently. Discuss the visual expectation before deciding the scope."],["Corners and junctions need thought","Where one wall is newly skimmed and the next is not, the junction still needs a neat finish. That is normal detail work, but it is worth clarifying where the new surface starts and stops."],["Budget can be focused where it matters","If several rooms need work, spending on the genuinely poor surfaces first can make more sense than automatically resurfacing every wall. Ask for separate options if you want to compare."]],"takeaway":"Reskim the surfaces that need it. A whole-room finish is an option, not a rule.","related":["patch-repair-or-reskim","plaster-repair-cost-vs-reskim","is-cheapest-plastering-quote-best"]},
{"slug":"questions-to-ask-plasterer-before-hiring","category":"Choosing a Plasterer","title":"10 questions to ask a plasterer before you hire them","excerpt":"A better quote starts with better questions: scope, preparation, protection, timing, changes and what happens after the plastering is done.","image":'kc37',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Ask questions that reveal the proposed scope and working method, not just whether the plasterer is available. You want to understand what is being prepared, what is being finished, what is excluded and how changes will be agreed.","sections":[["1–3: What exactly are you proposing?","Ask which walls and ceilings are included, what preparation is allowed for, and whether damaged areas are being repaired, removed or simply covered. If the answer is 'we'll just skim it', ask what makes the existing background suitable."],["4–6: What do you need from me?","Ask how empty the room needs to be, what access is required and whether other trades need to finish first. Clarify who deals with radiators, sockets, fitted units, parking and any building restrictions."],["7–8: How do price changes work?","Ask what would legitimately change the quote and how additional work would be authorised. Existing walls can contain surprises; the important thing is having a process rather than pretending surprises cannot happen."],["9–10: What happens at handover?","Ask what condition the room will be left in and what you need to know about drying and decorating. Also confirm the contact route if you have a question after the work. The answers should make the project easier to picture before you commit."]],"takeaway":"The best questions expose the scope, preparation and handover. Ask those before asking whether somebody can start next week.","related":["choosing-a-plasterer","what-should-plastering-quote-include","plastering-quote-red-flags"]},
{"slug":"how-many-plastering-quotes","category":"Choosing a Plasterer","title":"How many plastering quotes should you get?","excerpt":"There is no magic number, but enough comparable quotes can show whether the scope and price assumptions make sense.","image":'kc38',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"For a meaningful job, getting more than one quote can help you compare scope and approach. The value comes from asking each plasterer to price the same surfaces and requirements—not collecting the largest possible pile of numbers.","sections":[["Two or three clear quotes can be more useful than six vague ones","If each contractor has seen the same information and explains the scope, you can compare method, exclusions and total. More quotes add little if every brief is different."],["Use one written brief","Send the same room list, photographs, measurements and known issues to each person. If one plasterer identifies an extra problem, ask the others whether they have considered it rather than silently comparing different jobs."],["Do not use quotes only to chase the lowest number","A quote is also a description of what the contractor believes the job needs. A higher figure may include preparation another quote omits; a lower figure may be an entirely suitable smaller scope. Ask what explains the difference."],["Stop when you understand the options","Once you have enough information to understand the likely scope, price range and contractor approach, more enquiries can become noise. Choose on the actual job and communication, not on quote-collecting as a sport."]],"takeaway":"Get enough quotes to compare scope confidently. Consistency of the brief matters more than hitting a particular number.","related":["is-cheapest-plastering-quote-best","what-to-send-for-a-quote","choosing-a-plasterer"]},
{"slug":"plastering-quote-red-flags","category":"Choosing a Plasterer","title":"Plastering quote red flags: what should make you ask another question?","excerpt":"Not every short quote is bad, but vague scope, unexplained extras and pressure to decide immediately deserve a closer look.","image":'kc39',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"A plastering quote deserves more scrutiny when you cannot tell what surfaces, preparation or materials it covers, when important exclusions are unclear, or when the contractor will not explain how changes are handled.","sections":[["A total with no identifiable scope","'Plaster room — £X' tells you very little if you do not know whether the ceiling, preparation, repairs or materials are included. Ask for enough detail that both sides can recognise the agreed job later."],["Problems are promised away without assessment","Be cautious if damp, loose plaster, active leaks or movement are dismissed as irrelevant without inspection. A cosmetic finish cannot make every underlying issue disappear."],["Extra work has no approval process","A quote does not need to predict every hidden defect, but you should know how additional work is explained and authorised. 'We'll sort it and tell you after' is not a clear process."],["Pressure replaces explanation","Availability can change quickly, but you should still be able to ask questions and understand what you are buying. A contractor who explains the trade-offs is easier to work with than one who treats clarification as an inconvenience."]],"takeaway":"A red flag is usually lack of clarity, not a particular price. Ask until the scope and change process make sense.","related":["what-should-plastering-quote-include","questions-to-ask-plasterer-before-hiring","is-cheapest-plastering-quote-best"]},
{"slug":"reviews-vs-photos-plasterer","category":"Choosing a Plasterer","title":"Reviews or project photos: what matters more when choosing a plasterer?","excerpt":"Photos show finishes. Reviews show experiences. Neither proves the next job will be right on its own.","image":'kc40',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Use reviews and project photos for different purposes. Photos can show the type of work and finish a plasterer presents; reviews can show patterns in communication and customer experience. The quote and conversation still need to fit your own project.","sections":[["What photos can tell you","Look for work relevant to yours: ceilings if you need ceilings, repair work if you have repairs, occupied homes if access matters. Wide room views are more informative than endless close-ups of a wet trowelled surface."],["What reviews can tell you","Repeated comments about reliability, communication, tidiness or handling changes can be useful. One glowing or angry review is less informative than a consistent pattern across several jobs."],["What neither can prove","A photo cannot show what preparation happened underneath, and a review cannot verify every technical detail. Do not let social proof replace a clear site-specific scope."],["Ask for relevance, not volume","If you have an unusual ceiling or refurbishment sequence, ask whether the plasterer has handled similar work and what the key considerations were. One relevant example can be more useful than fifty unrelated images."]],"takeaway":"Use photos to understand finish and relevance; use reviews to understand patterns of experience. Use the quote to understand your own job.","related":["choosing-a-plasterer","questions-to-ask-plasterer-before-hiring","what-should-plastering-quote-include"]},
{"slug":"what-should-plastering-quote-include","category":"Choosing a Plasterer","title":"What should a plastering quote include?","excerpt":"The room name and a total are not enough. A useful quote makes the scope, preparation, exclusions and change process understandable.","image":'kc41',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"A useful plastering quote should identify the surfaces being worked on, the main preparation and finish, what is included or excluded, the price basis and how any additional work will be agreed.","sections":[["The exact surfaces","List the rooms, walls and ceilings. If only selected walls are included, identify them. This avoids the classic problem where 'do the bedroom' means different things to each person."],["Preparation and method","The quote should give enough information to understand whether loose areas are being repaired, old surfaces retained, ceilings skimmed or overboarded, and what preparation is anticipated. It does not need to become a technical textbook."],["Practical responsibilities","Clarify room clearance, protection, parking, water, waste and the boundary with other trades. Confirm whether materials are included and whether VAT applies to the stated figure."],["Changes, payment and completion","Record how unforeseen work will be approved, any payment stages and what marks completion of the agreed plastering scope. Also discuss drying and decorating separately so they are not mistaken for part of the plasterer's finish date."]],"takeaway":"A good quote lets a third person understand what work was agreed. If the scope exists only in conversation, ask for it in writing.","related":["what-affects-plastering-quote","plastering-quote-red-flags","plasterer-day-rate-vs-fixed-price"]},
{"slug":"how-long-does-plastering-take-room","category":"Planning & Aftercare","title":"How long does it take to plaster a room?","excerpt":"Room size matters, but preparation, ceilings, repairs and drying mean the answer is rarely just 'one day'.","image":'kc42',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"The working time for a room depends on the surfaces included and preparation required. A straightforward wall skim is different from a room with ceiling work, repairs or failed backgrounds. Drying and decorating happen after the plastering work itself and need separate time.","sections":[["Define what 'plaster a room' means","One wall, four walls, walls plus ceiling and a room needing extensive preparation are different jobs. Ask for the expected working sequence based on the quoted scope rather than relying on a generic room time."],["Preparation can take longer than the visible finish","Protecting access, repairing backgrounds, boarding or dealing with awkward details can all happen before the final skim. That time is part of creating the surface you see at the end."],["Work duration and drying duration are not the same","A plasterer can finish the physical application while the room is still not ready to paint. Keep the plastering booking, drying period and decorating date as separate stages in your plan."],["Build in room for discoveries","Renovation work can expose damaged areas once loose material is removed. A good programme allows the scope to be confirmed without forcing the next trade into the room before the surface is ready."]],"takeaway":"Ask how long the quoted scope should take, then plan drying and decorating separately. 'One room' is not a standard unit of time.","related":["how-long-does-plaster-take-to-dry","decorate-after-plastering-timeline","planning-your-plastering-project"]},
{"slug":"how-long-does-plaster-take-to-dry","category":"Planning & Aftercare","title":"How long does new plaster take to dry?","excerpt":"There is no dependable single number for every wall. Thickness, background, room conditions and the product system all matter.","image":'kc43',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"New plaster should be allowed to dry appropriately before decorating, but a fixed number of days is not reliable for every job. Product, thickness, background, temperature and ventilation all influence drying.","sections":[["A skim and a deep repair are not the same","Thin finishing plaster and thicker areas of repair contain different amounts of moisture. One room can therefore dry unevenly even when everything was completed on the same day."],["Room conditions matter","Cold conditions slow drying. British Gypsum specifies minimum background temperatures for its gypsum plaster products and notes that longer setting and drying times should be allowed in low temperatures.","Ventilation also needs to be sensible rather than treating the room like a sealed box."],["Appearance is useful but not a universal test","Colour change can give an indication, but product instructions and actual conditions matter. If an area remains visibly different, feels suspect or was affected by a previous leak, do not paint simply because the calendar says enough days have passed."],["Coordinate with the decorator","Tell the decorator what plastering and repairs were carried out. The first paint system should follow the paint manufacturer's guidance for new plaster rather than a one-size-fits-all recipe."]],"takeaway":"Plan with flexibility. The plaster is ready when the actual surface and product guidance say it is ready—not when a generic countdown ends.","refs":["gypsumTemp","gypsum"],"related":["when-can-you-paint-new-plaster","heating-ventilation-new-plaster","decorate-after-plastering-timeline"]},
{"slug":"heating-ventilation-new-plaster","category":"Planning & Aftercare","title":"Should you use heating or open windows to dry new plaster?","excerpt":"The goal is steady, suitable drying—not trying to force the wall dry as fast as possible.","image":'kc44',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Keep the room within suitable temperature conditions and provide sensible ventilation, following the product and contractor guidance. Avoid extreme attempts to force-dry new plaster, and do not let the background freeze or stay below the product's stated minimum temperature.","sections":[["Cold rooms slow the process","British Gypsum states minimum temperatures for gypsum plaster backgrounds and notes that low temperatures require allowance for longer setting and drying. That matters in unheated renovations during colder weather."],["Ventilation helps moisture leave the room","Normal ventilation can help remove moisture released during drying. The aim is not a powerful draught across one wall while the rest of the room is cold; it is a stable environment where moisture can escape."],["Do not improvise with intense direct heat","Placing strong heat directly against one area can create uneven conditions. Follow the plasterer's and product manufacturer's recommendations for the system used rather than trying to win a race against the decorating date."],["Plan heating before the job in winter","If a property has no working heating, mention it at quote stage. Temporary arrangements may need considering before plastering, especially where the building is very cold."]],"takeaway":"Aim for steady temperature and sensible ventilation. Drying plaster is a controlled stage of the job, not something to force overnight.","refs":["gypsumTemp"],"related":["how-long-does-plaster-take-to-dry","when-can-you-paint-new-plaster","decorate-after-plastering-timeline"]},
{"slug":"can-you-live-in-house-during-plastering","category":"Planning & Aftercare","title":"Can you stay in the house while plastering is being done?","excerpt":"Often yes for smaller projects, but access, dust, room closures, children, pets and the number of rooms can change the answer.","image":'kc45',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Many people remain at home during limited plastering work, but whether it is practical depends on the rooms involved, access routes and household needs. Larger or multi-room work can make temporary arrangements more comfortable.","sections":[["Think beyond the room being plastered","Materials and tools need to travel through hallways, stairs and entrances. If the bathroom, kitchen or main route is involved, the disruption can be greater than the size of the work area suggests."],["Protect children and pets from the work zone","Keep them away from tools, wet materials and protected access routes. Do not rely on a dust sheet as a barrier. Agree which areas are out of bounds while work is active."],["Working from home needs planning","Noise, movement and temporary room closures can interrupt calls and concentration. Tell the plasterer if you need uninterrupted access to a particular room or entrance so the sequence can be discussed."],["For larger projects, consider staging","Completing rooms or floors in stages can make an occupied home easier to manage, although staging may affect programme and cost. Ask what sequence is practical rather than assuming every room should be started at once."]],"takeaway":"Staying home is often possible, but plan the routes and room closures—not just the wall being plastered.","related":["how-messy-is-plastering","prepare-a-room-for-plastering","planning-your-plastering-project"]},
{"slug":"decorate-after-plastering-timeline","category":"Planning & Aftercare","title":"Plastering to decorating: what should the timeline look like?","excerpt":"Finish day is not paint day. Build your programme around drying, checking the surface and the paint manufacturer's instructions.","image":'kc46',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Treat plastering, drying and decorating as separate stages. Once the plaster is suitably dry, the decorator can prepare and paint it using a system appropriate for new plaster.","sections":[["Stage 1: plastering complete","At handover, ask what work was carried out and whether any areas were deeper repairs or different materials. Keep those details for the decorator."],["Stage 2: allow the surface to dry","Do not book paint solely by counting from the final plastering day. Thickness, temperature and background influence drying, so the actual surface needs to be ready."],["Stage 3: inspect before covering","If you see persistent damp-looking areas, movement or another problem, ask before painting. Paint should not become the method for hiding uncertainty about the background."],["Stage 4: use the correct paint preparation","Follow the paint manufacturer's instructions for new plaster, including any recommended primer or first-coat preparation. Avoid universal internet recipes that ignore the specific paint product."]],"takeaway":"Separate the trades in your calendar: plaster first, drying second, decoration third. Leave enough flexibility for the actual wall conditions.","refs":["gypsum"],"related":["when-can-you-paint-new-plaster","how-long-does-plaster-take-to-dry","heating-ventilation-new-plaster"]},
{"slug":"plastering-before-or-after-kitchen-fit","category":"Planning & Aftercare","title":"Should plastering happen before or after a new kitchen is fitted?","excerpt":"Usually the cleaner sequence is to complete the wall preparation before final units and finishes, but first-fix services and project details can change the order.","image":'kc47',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"In many kitchen refurbishments, plastering is planned after the necessary first-fix service work and before final kitchen installation and decoration. The exact order depends on the design, wall condition and which surfaces will remain accessible.","sections":[["Get disruptive service work done first","Electrical and plumbing changes that open or chase walls should normally be coordinated before the final plaster finish. Otherwise newly finished surfaces can be cut into again."],["Know where units and splashbacks will go","The plasterer needs to understand the kitchen layout, especially where surfaces disappear behind units or require particular preparation for later finishes. Do not assume every hidden wall needs the same decorative standard."],["Protect the finish from the installation stage","Kitchen fitting still involves drilling, fixing and moving large items. Build in sensible protection and allow the plaster to dry before later decorating or finish-sensitive work."],["Sequence beats speed","Rushing plastering in before the service layout is final can create more making good. Share the kitchen plan and trade schedule so the wall work supports the project rather than fighting it."]],"takeaway":"Coordinate first-fix services, then plaster the agreed surfaces before final kitchen fitting wherever practical. Use the actual kitchen plan to set the sequence.","related":["planning-your-plastering-project","plastering-before-or-after-electrics","prepare-a-room-for-plastering"]},
{"slug":"plastering-before-or-after-electrics","category":"Planning & Aftercare","title":"Should plastering be done before or after electrical work?","excerpt":"First-fix electrical work and chasing normally need coordinating before the final plaster finish; final accessories come later.","image":'kc48',"published":"2026-10-02","answer":"Where electrical work requires new cables, boxes or chases, coordinate that first-fix work before the final plastering. Final fitting of accessories is then arranged around the finished and suitably dry surface by a competent electrician.","sections":[["First fix can disturb the wall","New cable routes, back boxes and alterations may require cutting or chasing. Doing that after a completed skim usually creates avoidable damage and making good."],["Agree who makes good around chases and boxes","Do not assume the electrician or plasterer has included every repair. The quote should identify where damaged surfaces are being filled, bonded or finished."],["Keep electrical safety with the electrician","Do not remove sockets or alter wiring yourself simply to help the plasterer. Use a competent electrician for electrical work and coordinate isolation or accessory removal where required."],["Final fit needs the right timing","Accessories, faceplates and final decoration should be sequenced so the new plaster can dry and the wall can be finished cleanly. Share dates between trades rather than booking each in isolation."]],"takeaway":"Coordinate electrical first fix before the final plaster finish, then arrange final electrical fitting afterwards. Make the boundary between trades explicit.","related":["planning-your-plastering-project","plastering-before-or-after-kitchen-fit","prepare-a-room-for-plastering"]}
];
